# SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000078  -  CAT II
# Web server accounts not utilized by installed features (i.e., tools,
# utilities, specific services, etc.) must not be created and must be
# deleted when the web server feature is uninstalled.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000078
group:           V-206378
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-000381
control_rev5:    CM-7

contribution:    The one account the Splunk installation itself creates is the
                 administrator defined in user-seed.conf at first start; the application
                 can show that no such seeded account definition remains in the
                 configuration.

governs:         user-seed.conf - The initial administrator account created on a first start.

check:           user-seed.conf [user_info] USERNAME absent

external:        Enumeration and removal of accounts in Splunk's own user store, which is
                 held in the instance's user data rather than in a .conf file an assessor
                 can read, so it is reviewed through the user administration interface.
external:        Operating system accounts created by the installation package, carried by
                 the operating system.
external:        Account inventory in the directory service where authentication is
                 external, carried by that service.

citation:        Web Server SRG SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000078 check and fix text;
                 user-seed.conf.spec:25 ([user_info]), user-seed.conf.spec:28 (USERNAME)
finding:         A seeded installation account definition remains in user-seed.conf, so the
                 application still carries an account created by the installation process
                 rather than by an installed feature in use.
